Erik Hesselink wrote:
> 
> I was referring more to the concern in general voiced in this thread and 
> an earlier thread that conducts in themselves are too spoily, since they 
> might give an unspoiled player ideas what to try. For example, a 
> hornless conduct that says 'never applied a unicorn horn' might make an 
> unspoiled player think 'hey, I should try applying a unicorn horn next 
> time'. However, in this case there is clearly no time in the game where 
> you could start including this conduct (like in the above Medusa 
> example) other than ascension. The same goes for people who think 'never 
> wished for an artifact' is too spoily, since it tips you to the 
> existance of both wishes and artifacts.
 
I don't see it as very spoily.  In game, all the conduct needs to say
is "hornless".  It doesn't need to explain that this means you never
applied a unicorn horn.  That would not spoil the actual use of
unicorn horns much at all.  Back before I knew what unicorn horns were
for, I did guess that they were valuable.  I noticed that monsters
like to have them, and that they prevented status effects for
monsters.  But I just assumed they were a weapon, and perhaps had a
chance to prevent effects passively.  Eventually I gave up on them.

On a tangent, once I was spoiled about unicorn horns, I think it made
a big difference.  I almost immediately went from 0 ascensions in 4
years to averaging about 1 ascension in 15 games.  That was also the
time I started using a version with sokoban in it, so I'm sure it's
not the only factor.  But I do think this conduct would make a bigger
difference than some people give it credit.

Why not solve the problem of spoily conducts by adding in lots of
conducts, some of which aren't challenging at all.  It could be
humorous to look at the list of silly conducts, and fun to try to
figure out what some of them really mean.

